---
import Guide from "../../../layouts/Guide.astro"
import Code from "../../../components/Code.astro"
import DocLink from "../../../components/DocLink.astro"
import Builds from "./partials/Builds.astro"
import CssFiles from "./partials/CssFiles.astro"
import InstallCommand from "./partials/InstallCommand.astro"
import NextSteps from "./partials/NextSteps.astro"
import PickAndChoose from "./partials/PickAndChoose.astro"
import QuickStart from "./partials/QuickStart.astro"
import ServerSupport from "./partials/ServerSupport.astro"

export const keywords = ["install", "npm", "setup", "ssr"]
---

<Guide overline="Guide">
  <Fragment slot="title">Getting started</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    OPUI ships first-class Solid 2 components. Install the package, import what
    you need, and you're set.
  </Fragment>

  <section id="quick-start">
    <h2>Quick start</h2>
    <QuickStart>
      <Fragment slot="install">
        <InstallCommand packages="solid-js@2 @solidjs/web@2" />
        <Code
          code={`// src/index.tsx
import "opui-css/css/imports.css"`}
          lang="tsx"
        />
      </Fragment>
      <Code
        slot="use"
        code={`import { Button } from "opui-css/solid"

export default function App() {
  return <Button color="primary" variant="filled">Save</Button>
}`}
        lang="tsx"
      />
    </QuickStart>
  </section>

  <section id="whats-in-the-css">
    <h2>What's in the CSS</h2>
    <CssFiles />
  </section>

  <section id="css">
    <h2>Import only what you use</h2>
    <p>
      List the files in a CSS file, and import that file in your entry instead
      of <code>imports.css</code>:
    </p>
    <PickAndChoose file="src/styles/main.css" />
    <Code
      code={`// src/index.tsx
import "./styles/main.css"`}
      lang="tsx"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="builds">
    <h2>Builds</h2>
    <p>
      The components ship as <code>.jsx</code> files with their types, which
      <code>@solidjs/vite-plugin</code> compiles with your Solid through the
      package's <code>solid</code> export condition, for server rendering too.
    </p>
    <Builds tools={["vite"]} />
  </section>

  <section id="typescript">
    <h2>TypeScript</h2>
    <p>
      The types work with TypeScript 6 and 7, with <code>moduleResolution</code>
      set to <code>"bundler"</code> and <code>jsxImportSource</code> set to
      <code>"@solidjs/web"</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="usage">
    <h2>How to use</h2>
    <p>
      Slots are props: <code>children</code>, and camelCased names like
      <code>endText</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <DocLink href="/components/field">Field</DocLink> wraps your own
      control. Give the control an <code>id</code>, set <code>for</code> on the
      field to it, and bind it like any input. For a hint, set the control's
      <code>aria-describedby</code> to <code>&lt;id&gt;-end-text</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="element">
    <h2>Reaching the element</h2>
    <p>
      Every component hands <code>ref</code> to its root element, typed for it:
      a <code>Dialog</code> ref is an <code>HTMLDialogElement</code>. Most of
      the time you need none: a <code>Button</code> with
      <code>command="show-modal"</code> and <code>commandfor</code> opens a
      dialog by its <code>id</code>, and its click handler can set your state at
      the same time. When a script opens it, such as after a check, reach the
      element:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`import { Button, Dialog, DialogContent } from "opui-css/solid"

export function Confirm() {
  let dialog: HTMLDialogElement | undefined

  return (
    <>
      <Button onClick={() => dialog?.showModal()}>Delete</Button>
      <Dialog
        aria-label="Delete the file?"
        id="confirm"
        ref={(element) => (dialog = element)}
      >
        <DialogContent>The file goes to the bin for 30 days.</DialogContent>
      </Dialog>
    </>
  )
}`}
      lang="tsx"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="ssr">
    <h2>Server rendering</h2>
    <p>
      Every component renders full HTML on the server and hydrates. Solid 2
      renders on the server with the <code>start</code> and <code>ssr</code>
      options of <code>@solidjs/vite-plugin</code>, which need no setting for
      the package:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`// vite.config.ts
import solid from "@solidjs/vite-plugin"
import { defineConfig } from "vite"

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [solid({ ssr: true, start: true })],
})`}
      lang="ts"
    />
    <p>
      A page with more than one root gives each its own <code>renderId</code> in
      <code>renderToString</code> and <code>hydrate</code>, so the ids the
      components generate differ between the roots. Roots hydrated in the same
      task each claim their server markup. A root hydrated in a later task
      renders again over its markup, so load the code of every root first and
      call <code>hydrate</code> for all of them in one loop.
    </p>
    <p>The props in the last column need hydration.</p>
    <p>
      A choice the user makes before the page hydrates stays on screen, as on a
      plain Solid input: Solid doesn't call <code>onChange</code> or
      <code>onValueChange</code> for it, and a controlled value misses it. A
      form that reads its values on submit gets it.
    </p>
    <ServerSupport framework="solid" />
  </section>

  <section id="next-steps">
    <h2>Next steps</h2>
    <NextSteps />
  </section>
</Guide>
